bodyText:
Guam's eviction chapter lets you recover possession when a tenant:
- stays after the lease term ends without your permission (a tenancy at will must first be ended by notice);
- doesn't pay rent after a 5-day written notice to pay or leave;
- breaks another lease term and doesn't comply within 3 days after a written notice to comply or leave (no notice is needed for a breach that can't afterward be cured);
- or assigns, sublets or commits waste against the lease, which ends the lease on a 5-day notice to quit.

The Rental Act adds its own steps for residential leases: 5 working days to pay after a nonpayment notice, with termination no sooner than 30 working days after the tenant receives it; and 5 working days after a breach notice, with no termination if a curable breach is fixed in time. Meet the longer Rental Act periods.

Other Rental Act grounds: refusing lawful access lets you end the lease.

Limits: you may not evict a victim because of family violence, criminal sexual conduct or stalking; an oral lease of a primary residence can't be ended in its first year except for nonpayment or damage.

Guam gives tenants several ways to end a lease early without owing the rest of the term:
- a victim of family violence, criminal sexual conduct or stalking who gives written notice and the required evidence (they owe one month's rent, due within 90 working days after moving out);
- a tenant whose unit you don't deliver on time (at least 5 working days' written notice);
- a tenant whose unit is substantially damaged by fire or casualty they didn't cause;
- a tenant you unlawfully lock out or whose essential services you willfully cut;
- a tenant whose written notice of your breach of the lease, or of your code and repair duties affecting health and safety, you don't cure within 14 working days or show reason why the date in the notice can't be met (the lease then ends on the date in the notice, at least 30 working days after you receive it);
- and a tenant facing unlawful or harassing entries.

Servicemembers have federal rights. Otherwise a tenant who leaves early is liable for rent, but you must try to re-rent at a fair rental, and your recovery is capped (the lesser of the rest of the term's rent, or the re-renting period's rent plus a reasonable commission plus any shortfall).

A fixed early-termination charge is a sum fixed in advance, valid only if actual damage would be impracticable to fix; the library's optional clause (`early-termination-gu`) states that agreement, but a court decides.
